# Containerfile, `.sops` Schema
#
# This file is a Containerfile (Dockerfile) that shows how to place the sops
# binary into a service image. It is not executed as-is: replace the angle
# bracket placeholders with the real base and application images.

## File Format

Standard Containerfile/Dockerfile syntax, one instruction per line, comments
with `#`. Instructions used here:

| Instruction | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| `FROM <image> AS <stage>` | Start a build stage from a base image, optionally named |
| `COPY --from=<stage-or-image> <src> <dst>` | Copy files from another build stage or from any image (no stage needed) |
| `RUN <cmd>` | Run a command at build time (needs a shell in that stage) |
| `ENTRYPOINT ["exec", "form"]` | JSON array form: no shell interpolation, the process runs directly |
| `ENTRYPOINT ["sh", "-c", "..."]` | Shell form via an explicit `sh -c`: required here because `sops exec-env` needs a shell |

## Stage Model

`FROM ghcr.io/getsops/sops:<version>-alpine AS sops` pulls the upstream image
that ships a statically linked `sops` at `/usr/local/bin/sops`. It exists only
as a **source of that binary**, the final image does not inherit anything else
from it.

Variant A keeps the application's own base image and copies the binary in
(`COPY --from=sops ...`). The `--from=` reference works with a named stage.

Variant B inverts that: the final image **is** the sops image (so it has
busybox `sh`), and the application's binary plus its shared libraries are
copied in from the application image.

## Constraints

- The final image MUST contain a POSIX shell (`/bin/sh`); `sops exec-env`
  execs the application through `/bin/sh -c`, so a distroless or scratch base
  cannot be used unless the entrypoint is changed to a shell-free form (which
  `exec-env` does not support).
- The application command is passed as the `$APP_CMD` environment variable (or
  written literally into the shell string); quoting inside the JSON array form
  is significant, the inner `\"` characters are how a quote survives the JSON
  array into the shell command.
- The sops version tag is a parameter (`P-7`); pin it to a real release
  (`v3.11.0` is the version this schematic was reverse-engineered against).
- Variant B loses the application base image's package manager expectations:
  anything the app needs at runtime (`ca-certificates` for TLS, `tzdata`,
  locale files) must be re-added with `apk`.

## Placeholders to Replace

| Placeholder | Meaning | Example |
|-------------|---------|---------|
| `<your-base-image>` | The application's existing base | `python:3.12-slim` |
| `<application-image>` | The image that currently builds the app | `myorg/app:1.4.2` |
| `<app>` | The application binary name | `myservice` |
| `<app-deps>` | Shared libraries/paths from the original image | `x86_64-linux-gnu` |
